  secToolsTitle:"Eight tools, <em>15 minutes of Taiwan Mandarin a day</em>.",
  secPersonaTitle:"Three kinds of learners — <em>an entrance for each</em>.",
  ps1t:"For travel", ps1d:"Order \"half sugar, less ice\" at the night market, tell the taxi to turn right. The <b>travel-filtered phrasebook</b> and Level 1 flashcards make your next Taiwan trip way more fun. No exam needed — start with your ears and mouth.",
  ps2t:"Living in Taiwan", ps2d:"NHI cards, landlords, garbage trucks — <b>the vocabulary of real Taiwan life</b> that textbooks skip. Level 2–3 plus SRS review and listening-included mock exams build ears that actually understand.",
  ps3t:"Applying to a Taiwanese university", ps3d:"Most universities look for <b>TOCFL Level 3 (B1)</b>. Level-by-level vocab and grammar plus mock exams with listening and reading let you track where you stand. Start with the <a href='tocfl.html' style='color:var(--ac)'>TOCFL Guide</a>.",
  secAboutTitle:"Made by a Taiwanese — <em>in reverse</em>.",
  story1:"I'm a Taiwanese engineer working in Japan. I built a study site for my own JLPT prep — StayJP Study — released it, and a lot of people ended up using it.",
  story2:"That's when I realized: <strong>just as I was learning Japanese, plenty of people out there want to learn Taiwan's language</strong>. So this time, the other direction. As a native speaker, I've collected the Chinese actually used in Taiwan — traditional characters, zhuyin, and everyday talk overflowing with 不好意思.",
  story3:"Not textbook-perfect Mandarin, but <strong>words that work on a Taipei street corner</strong>. Let's start from ordering a drink at the night market.",
  secStatsTitle:"Content keeps <em>growing</em>.",
  faqQ1:"Traditional characters, not simplified?",
  faqA1:"Yes. Taiwan uses <strong>traditional characters</strong>, and everything here follows Taiwanese usage — pronunciation, vocabulary, phrasing — including quirks like reading 和 as hàn.",
  faqQ2:"Zhuyin (ㄅㄆㄇㄈ) or pinyin — which should I learn?",
  faqA2:"Every word shows both, so either works. If you're comfortable with the Latin alphabet, pinyin is enough. If you want to match Taiwanese keyboards and local materials, learn zhuyin.",
  faqQ3:"Do I need to register or pay?",
  faqA3:"You can start right away without signing up. All <strong>content (words, grammar, phrases) is free forever</strong>. Practice tools (quiz, review, mock exams, speaking) have a daily free quota; unlimited use comes with Premium in the iOS app (¥980/mo, ¥5,800/yr, or ¥12,800 lifetime).",
  faqQ4:"What is TOCFL?",
  faqA4:"Taiwan's official Chinese proficiency test. It's the Taiwanese counterpart of the JLPT, and this site's Levels 1–5 are aligned to it. Most Taiwanese universities look for around Level 3 (B1). See the <a href='tocfl.html'>TOCFL Guide</a>.",
